sections.s2_technical:
An Entity is annotated with Entity and mapped to a table. It is managed by the JPA persistence context, so when it is attached, changes to its fields are automatically flushed to the database. It has an identity column, often relationships to other entities, and lifecycle callbacks. Because it is tied to the schema and the persistence context, you keep it internal. Exposing it risks leaking columns, coupling the API to the schema, and triggering lazy-loading errors when a serialiser touches an unloaded relationship outside a transaction.

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class Account {
    @Id private String id;
    private String holderName;
    @Version private long version;        // optimistic locking
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "account") private List<Transaction> txns; // lazy
    // managed: setting a field inside a transaction auto-updates the row
}
```

A DTO is a plain class, frequently a Java record for immutability, whose purpose is to move data. It is not managed by JPA and has no database identity. It commonly carries validation annotations for request shapes and defines the exact fields exposed to clients. It holds no business logic; it just carries data.

```java
public record TransferRequest(
        @NotBlank String from,
        @NotBlank String to,
        @DecimalMin("0.01") BigDecimal amount) {} // validation, no logic
```

A POJO is the broadest term: any plain Java object not tied to a framework. An Entity is technically a POJO with JPA annotations, and a DTO is a POJO used for transport, but when people distinguish POJO specifically, they usually mean a framework-free domain object that may contain business logic. For example, a Money value object or a domain model that enforces rules in its methods.

```java
// A framework-free POJO with business logic (a value object).
public class Money {
    private final BigDecimal amount;
    public Money(BigDecimal amount) {
        if (amount.scale() > 2) throw new IllegalArgumentException("max 2 decimals");
        this.amount = amount;
    }
    public Money add(Money o) { return new Money(this.amount.add(o.amount)); } // logic
}
```

When to use each is driven by the layer. In the persistence layer and repository, you use Entities, because that is what JPA manages. Across the API boundary and between layers, you use DTOs, because they are a stable, safe, validated contract decoupled from the schema. For framework-free domain logic and value objects, you use POJOs, because keeping business rules free of framework annotations makes them easy to unit test and reuse.

The flow in a typical request is: the controller receives a request DTO, the service maps it toward the domain, uses entities for persistence through the repository, performs logic possibly using POJO value objects, then maps an entity to a response DTO to return. The entity never escapes the service boundary.

A senior nuance: the mistake juniors make is collapsing all three into the entity, adding validation annotations to the entity and returning it directly, which couples persistence, transport, and validation into one class. That breaks decoupling, leaks fields, and creates lazy-loading bugs. Keeping the three roles separate — entity for the database, DTO for the wire, POJO for framework-free logic — is a hallmark of a well-layered application, and interviewers look for whether you understand why the separation exists, not just that it does.

